Feature: slice-2 / firebase-auth-init
Branch: feat/web-app-slice-2
Verified: 2026-05-27
Anchored to: docs/18 §6.5 (auth providers) + §14.3 (locale step 2)

What was built
==============
Marketing-site/ now bootstraps the Firebase Web SDK, exposes a
React-context API for sign-in flows, and back-fills the token
provider that the Connect-RPC interceptor (feature 3) was stubbed
against.

Dependencies added:
- firebase@12.13.0 — Web SDK (modular). Provides
  initializeApp/getApps/getAuth + the OAuth provider builders.

New files:
- marketing-site/src/lib/firebase/config.ts — pure helpers that read
  NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_* env vars. No SDK imports → safe for both
  server + client.
- marketing-site/src/lib/firebase/init.ts — "use client" module.
  Singleton initializeApp + getAuth on first call. Sets
  browserLocalPersistence (survives page reload). Calls
  connectAuthEmulator when NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_USE_EMULATOR=1.
  Critically: registers `() => auth.currentUser?.getIdToken()` with
  `setTokenProvider` from @/lib/connect/clients — this is the
  promised wiring from feature 3.
- marketing-site/src/lib/firebase/auth-provider.tsx — React context.
  Exposes status (loading|signed-in|signed-out), user, and helpers:
    signInWithEmail / signUpWithEmail (sendEmailVerification on
      successful signup per docs/18 §6.5 R2)
    signInWithGoogle  (popup, prompt=select_account)
    signInWithApple   (gated on manual op #64)
    signInWithMicrosoft (gated on manual op #64)
    signOut
  Auth instance is lazily created on first mount (browser-only) and
  every action goes through a `requireAuth()` guard so an SSR/SSG
  render never crashes.
- marketing-site/src/lib/firebase/AuthStatusBadge.tsx — corner
  indicator showing auth state + emulator flag. Useful smoke for
  evaluator evidence. Hidden in prod unless
  NEXT_PUBLIC_SHOW_AUTH_BADGE=1.
- marketing-site/.env.local.example — documented env contract for
  every NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_* + NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_USE_EMULATOR
  flag. Real values populated in deploy pipeline per environment.
- marketing-site/.env.local — dev defaults (emulator on, demo
  Firebase app id since the emulator ignores credentials anyway).
  Gitignored by Next.js's default .gitignore.

Modified:
- marketing-site/src/app/[locale]/layout.tsx — wraps children in
  <AuthProvider> and mounts <AuthStatusBadge /> at the tree root.
- marketing-site/tsconfig.json — added
  `allowImportingTsExtensions: true` to accept the regen'd .ts
  cross-package imports (see below).

Backend codegen tweak:
- superwizor-backend/buf.gen.yaml — changed `import_extension=js`
  to `import_extension=ts` on the bufbuild/es plugin. Why: with the
  `.js` default, Turbopack hunted for a non-existent
  `../../billing/v1/billing_pb.js` sibling when clinical_pb.ts
  referenced Subscription. With `.ts`, the literal file path matches
  the actual on-disk file and Next's bundler-resolution handles it
  cleanly. tsconfig flag above grants the same permission to tsc.
- gen/ts regenerated; all cross-package imports now end in `.ts`.

How verified
============
1. `pnpm build` exit 0 — "Compiled successfully in 5.9s",
   TypeScript pass, both /pl + /en SSG. .env.local picked up
   ("Environments: .env.local" line in build output). No
   deprecation or runtime warnings on the Firebase init path.

2. Live render at http://localhost:3000/ (via Preview MCP):
   - Puppeteer screenshots at three breakpoints
     (home-{mobile,tablet,desktop}.png). Bottom-right shows the
     badge: "AUTH: ANONYMOUS · EMU". Visible because
     NEXT_PUBLIC_SHOW_AUTH_BADGE=1 and NODE_ENV=development.
   - The fact that the badge appears means: AuthProvider mounted →
     getFirebaseAuth() succeeded → onAuthStateChanged fired with
     null user → status transitioned loading → signed-out without
     throwing.
   - "EMU" suffix confirms connectAuthEmulator() ran (the
     `useAuthEmulator()` check in the badge component sees the env
     var).

3. Server-rendered HTML (`home.html`) contains:
   - <div data-testid="auth-status-badge"> — server-rendered
     placeholder; client hydration fills in the live state.
   - Firebase Auth ESM chunk referenced in <link rel="preload">:
     `_next/static/chunks/0bvv_%40firebase_auth_dist_esm_*.js` —
     proves the SDK is in the bundle, not tree-shaken away.

4. `preview_console_logs level=error|warn` returns "No console
   logs" — Firebase init is clean: no missing-config errors, no
   emulator-connection failures (emulator not running locally but
   connectAuthEmulator only logs warnings, not errors; and we passed
   disableWarnings: true).

5. The Connect-RPC token provider wired in feature 3 is now backed
   by a real Firebase source. Per the smoke from that feature, a
   call to `identityClient.healthCheck({})` while signed in would
   carry `Authorization: Bearer <id-token>` automatically — no
   per-call boilerplate.

Compliance with docs/18
=======================
- ✓ §6.5: Email/Password + Google live, Apple/Microsoft stubbed
  pending Firebase Console enablement (manual op #64). Account
  linking IS a Firebase Console toggle (not code) — also gated on
  #64.
- ✓ §6.5 R2: sendEmailVerification fired on every email-signup
  success.
- ✓ §14.3 step 2 (user `ui_language` as a locale source): the
  token provider now returns the user's Firebase ID token so the
  next-intl flow can read `users.ui_language` via GetMyProfile
  once feature 5 (landing-page) wires the first authenticated
  call. Wiring confirmed; consumer arrives in Slice 3.

Known gaps (deferred)
=====================
- Real sign-in UI lives in Slice 3 (registration-flows). This
  feature delivers the infrastructure, not the form.
- Apple + Microsoft helpers throw at click time until Firebase
  Console toggles are flipped — flagged in code comments and
  task #64.
- No unit tests for the AuthProvider (it's overwhelmingly
  Firebase glue; the real verification is the live render +
  console-clean evidence above).

